  "verification": {
    "status": "measured",
    "label": "Measured on 8×H100 with experts-first backward prefetch",
    "implementation": "TorchTitan v0.2.0 Qwen3 MoE with separate outer-layer and full-expert FSDP2 units",
    "workload": "Batch 1 × 4096 synthetic tokens per rank; BF16; 2 layers; 128 experts; top-8; EP=1; expert FSDP=8; deterministic balanced round-robin expert assignment.",
    "measurement": "Clean rank-0 collective timing correlated with a separate FQN/module-labeled Nsight capture and Perfetto review.",
    "timeline": "Forward uses non-expert → experts. Backward uses experts → non-expert for both layers."
  },
  "description": [
    "This is the EP=1 counterpart to the expert-FSDP + EP challenge. Every rank processes its own data-parallel batch and there are no dispatch/combine all-to-alls. Local MoE remains router → permute → grouped SwiGLU → weighted unpermute.",
    "Each layer's dense attention/router state and routed-expert state are wrapped separately with FSDP=8. The 128-expert BF16 module is 4.5 GiB: its all-gather transfers 3.9375 GiB per rank and remains exposed long after attention finishes.",
    "Forward gathers each outer non-expert layer before its experts. Backward reverses parameter use: expert weights are gathered before attention, norm, and router parameters.",
    "Backward is even more communication-bound. Each FP32 expert-gradient reduce-scatter moves 7.875 GiB per rank and measured 23.7–23.8 ms. The serialized reduction stream delays the next dense RS and therefore the next layer's backward.",
    "TorchTitan v0.2.0 normally folds experts into the layer FSDP state when EP=1. This controlled benchmark uses the same FSDP2 primitives and Qwen implementation but wraps experts separately to expose production-relevant dense/expert prefetch granularity.",
    "Routing uses TorchTitan's debug balanced round-robin assignment so every expert receives the same number of synthetic token copies. This makes expert compute and communication deterministic; it does not model natural router imbalance."
  ],
